.TH SHIKISHA-SERVE 1 "2026-09-11" "SHIKISHA" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shikisha-serve \- SHIKISHA with no window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shikisha-serve
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opens the terminals named in its settings, watches what the programs in them
are doing, fires the automation they trigger, and serves the board \(em the
screen you would otherwise be sitting in front of \(em over the network, to a
browser, a phone, or a laptop somewhere else.
.PP
It is the same runtime the windowed program on Windows runs, given nothing to
draw with. Meant for a machine nobody is sitting at: a server, a cloud VM.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and the commit it was built from.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary and exit.
.SH FILES
Everything it is given \(em settings, data, logs, the workspaces you define \(em
lives under one folder, chosen in this order:
.TP
.B $SHIKISHA_HOME
If set. This is how two of them run on one machine.
.TP
.I beside the program
If a
.B config
folder is sitting there. An unpacked copy keeps its settings with it.
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/shikisha
Otherwise \(em
.IR ~/.local/share/shikisha .
An installed copy cannot write beside itself, so this is where it goes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHIKISHA_HOME
Where to keep everything, overriding the choice above.
.SH SERVICE
A systemd
.I user
unit is installed, and not enabled: starting something that keeps running is
the machine owner's decision.
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now shikisha
loginctl enable\-linger "$USER"
.RE
.fi
.PP
The second line is what keeps it running after you log out.
.SH SECURITY
The board is served with a token in its address, and the remote is off until
you turn it on. Anyone holding that address can drive every terminal it shows.
.SH SEE ALSO
The manual, and everything this can be told to do:
.UR https://github.com/styleio/ShikishaTerm
.UE
